ypes or something, but for Half Flush, the pair must be dot or honor, so W2 pair is not allowed. So I must discard W2 eventually. So perhaps discard W2 now to improve efficiency? But discarding a pair reduces my chance to have a ready pair later. I have other pairs: B4 and B8, so I could use one of those as the final pair. So discarding W2 might be okay. But W2 has 4 unseen, so there's chance to make a pung if I draw another, but for Half Flush, I don't want characters. So discard W2. However, looking at valid actions: Play W3; Play B7; Play F2; Play B8; Play B5; Play B2; Play B4; Play W2. I cannot discard W2? Wait, \"Play W2\" is a valid action, so I can discard W2. So I have both W2 and W3 as options. Let's evaluate a few promising moves:  \n1. Play W3: Discard the single character, keep the W2 pair for now, maintain dots and honor. This keeps flexibility for either Half Flush (if I later discard W2) or mixed hand.  \n2. Play W2: Discard the character pair, reduce clutter, fully commit to dots and honor for Half Flush. But lose a pair.  \n3. Play B7: Discard a scarce terminal dot, keep character tiles, might aim for All Types or something else.  \n4. Play F2: Discard the honor, aim for All Simples or something, but I have B1 terminal pung, so All Simples impossible.  \n\nGiven the tile counts and melds of others: Next player has chows in W and T and B6 pung, across has chows in W and T, previous has W4 pung and J1 pung. Many terminals and honors are being used. F2 (South) is my seat wind, and 4 are unseen, so there's chance to draw another. B7 only 1 left, so unlikely to improve. W3 has 4 left, so could draw for pair or chow. But for Half Flush, I want to ditch characters. So discarding a character is good. Between W2 and W3, W3 is a single, so discarding it is more efficient (keep pairs). Also, W2 is a pair, which could be used for a chow if I get W1 or W4, but W1 and W4 are low. Better to keep the pair for now as it might become part of a meld or the final pair 